{"context": "published", "host": "www.josephsmithpapers.org", "showHeader": true, "footerStyle": "mini", "statusCode": 200, "uri": "letter-to-edward-partridge-5-december-1833", "page": "4", "is404": false, "gallery": [{"image": "/7633/7633_ms155_2_01_lb1_065_thumb.jpg", "page": "1", "label": "65", "href": "/paper-summary/letter-to-edward-partridge-5-december-1833/1"}, {"image": "/7633/7633_ms155_2_01_lb1_066_thumb.jpg", "page": "2", "label": "66", "href": "/paper-summary/letter-to-edward-partridge-5-december-1833/2"}, {"image": "/7633/7633_ms155_2_01_lb1_067_thumb.jpg", "page": "3", "label": "67", "href": "/paper-summary/letter-to-edward-partridge-5-december-1833/3"}, {"image": "/7633/7633_ms155_2_01_lb1_068_thumb.jpg", "page": "4", "label": "68", "href": "/paper-summary/letter-to-edward-partridge-5-december-1833/4"}, {"image": "/7633/7633_ms155_2_01_lb1_069_thumb.jpg", "page": "5", "label": "69", "href": "/paper-summary/letter-to-edward-partridge-5-december-1833/5"}, {"image": "/7633/7633_ms155_2_01_lb1_070_thumb.jpg", "page": "6", "label": "70", "href": "/paper-summary/letter-to-edward-partridge-5-december-1833/6"}], "tableOfContents": [], "summary": {"uri": "letter-to-edward-partridge-5-december-1833", "series": "JSPPD", "urlTitleValue": "JSPPD3", "documentSeriesTitle": "Letter to Edward Partridge, 5 December 1833", "editorialPageNumber": "68", "isPageNumberPresent": true, "searchUrl": "/published/jsp/searchable/papers/30494/30494-0004.xml", "clearText": "<html><head></head><body><div class=\"indent continuation wasptag\"> without,<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"17849562267647990452\"><a href=\"#17849562267647990452\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans26\"><span id=\"documenttrans26\">26</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">JS first made this point in a <a href=\"/paper-summary/letter-to-church-leaders-in-jackson-county-missouri-18-august-1833\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/letter-to-church-leaders-in-jackson-county-missouri-18-august-1833\" poptitle=\"Letter to Church Leaders in Jackson County,\nMissouri, 18 August 1833\">letter</a> dated 18 August 1833: “It is the will of the Lord that . . . not one foot of land perchased should be given to the enimies of God or sold to them.” JS dictated a <a href=\"/paper-summary/revelation-16-17-december-1833-dc-101\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/revelation-16-17-december-1833-dc-101\" poptitle=\"Revelation, 16–17 December 1833 [D&amp;C\n101]\">revelation</a> eleven days after this 5 December letter was written that gave the same directive: “It is my will that my people should claim and hold claim upon that which I have appointed unto them though they should not be permited to dwell thereon.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/letter-to-church-leaders-in-jackson-county-missouri-18-august-1833\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/letter-to-church-leaders-in-jackson-county-missouri-18-august-1833\" poptitle=\"Letter to Church Leaders in Jackson County,\nMissouri, 18 August 1833\">Letter to Church Leaders in Jackson Co., MO, 18 Aug. 1833</a>; <a href=\"/paper-summary/revelation-16-17-december-1833-dc-101\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/revelation-16-17-december-1833-dc-101\" poptitle=\"Revelation, 16–17 December 1833 [D&amp;C\n101]\">Revelation, 16–17 Dec. 1833</a> [D&amp;C 101:99].)</p><hr></div></div></aside> evry exertion should be to maintain the cause you have espoused and to contribute to the necessities of one another as much as possable in this your great calamity and remember not to murmur at the dealings of God with his creature you are not as yet brought into as trying circumstances as were the ancient Prophets &amp; apostles Call to mind a Daniel<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"13865788007761886266\"><a href=\"#13865788007761886266\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans27\"><span id=\"documenttrans27\">27</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Daniel 6:4–23.</p><hr></div></div></aside> the three Hebrew Children,<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"4965941887120457407\"><a href=\"#4965941887120457407\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans28\"><span id=\"documenttrans28\">28</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Daniel chap. 3.</p><hr></div></div></aside> Jeremiah<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"12867055247209507899\"><a href=\"#12867055247209507899\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans29\"><span id=\"documenttrans29\">29</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Lamentations 3:1–21.</p><hr></div></div></aside> Paul<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"7054860857563530077\"><a href=\"#7054860857563530077\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans30\"><span id=\"documenttrans30\">30</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See 2 Corinthians 11:23–27.</p><hr></div></div></aside> Stephen<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"13715965915878995827\"><a href=\"#13715965915878995827\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans31\"><span id=\"documenttrans31\">31</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Acts 7:54–60.</p><hr></div></div></aside> and many more too numerous to mention who were stoned sawn asunder tempted slain with the sword and wandered about in sheep skins &amp; goat skins being destitute afflicted tormented of whom the world was not worthy. they wandered in deserts and in mountains and in dens and caves of the earth yet they all obtained a good report through faith<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"13482956336714878245\"><a href=\"#13482956336714878245\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans32\"><span id=\"documenttrans32\">32</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Hebrews 11:37–39.</p><hr></div></div></aside> and amidst all their afflictions they rejoiced that they were counted worthy to receive persecution for Christs sake<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"219908168184468538\"><a href=\"#219908168184468538\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans33\"><span id=\"documenttrans33\">33</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Acts 5:41.</p><hr></div></div></aside> we know not what we shall be called to pass through before <aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"6572482358157204596\"><a href=\"#6572482358157204596\" class=\"reference staticPopup\" ro=\"x4116\" topic=\"x4116\" title=\"Zion\">Zion</a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data\" isnote=\"Y\" isnonsearchable=\"Y\"><input type=\"hidden\" value=\"Zion\"><p>A specific location in Missouri; also a literal or figurative gathering of believers in Jesus Christ, characterized by adherence to ideals of harmony, equality, and purity. In JS’s earliest revelations “the cause of Zion” was used to broadly describe the ...</p><a class=\"more\" href=\"/topic/zion\">View Glossary</a></div></div></aside> is delivered and established therefore we have great need to live near to God and always be in strict obedience to all his <aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"16009710730706486340\"><a href=\"#16009710730706486340\" class=\"reference staticPopup\" ro=\"x6221\" topic=\"x6221\" title=\"Commandment\">commandments</a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data\" isnote=\"Y\" isnonsearchable=\"Y\"><input type=\"hidden\" value=\"Commandment\"><p>Generally, a divine mandate that church members were expected to obey; more specifically, a text dictated by JS in the first-person voice of Deity that served to communicate knowledge and instruction to JS and his followers. Occasionally, other inspired texts...</p><a class=\"more\" href=\"/topic/commandment\">View Glossary</a></div></div></aside> that we may have a concience void of offense towards God and man,<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"13642374065692674880\"><a href=\"#13642374065692674880\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans34\"><span id=\"documenttrans34\">34</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Acts 24:16.</p><hr></div></div></aside> It is your privelege to use every lawful means in your power to seek redress for your grievances of your enemies and prosecute them to the extent of the Law<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"1191261817470073014\"><a href=\"#1191261817470073014\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans35\"><span id=\"documenttrans35\">35</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See <a href=\"/paper-summary/letter-to-church-leaders-in-jackson-county-missouri-18-august-1833\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/letter-to-church-leaders-in-jackson-county-missouri-18-august-1833\" poptitle=\"Letter to Church Leaders in Jackson County,\nMissouri, 18 August 1833\">Letter to Church Leaders in Jackson Co., MO, 18 Aug. 1833</a>; and <a href=\"/paper-summary/revelation-16-17-december-1833-dc-101\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/revelation-16-17-december-1833-dc-101\" poptitle=\"Revelation, 16–17 December 1833 [D&amp;C\n101]\">Revelation, 16–17 Dec. 1833</a> [D&amp;C 101:81–89]; see also Historical Introduction to <a href=\"/paper-summary/letter-30-october-1833\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/letter-30-october-1833\" poptitle=\"Letter, 30 October 1833\">Letter, 30 Oct. 1833</a>.</p><hr></div></div></aside> but it will be impossable for us to render you any assistance in a temporal point of view as our means are already exhausted and are deeply in debt and know no means whereby we shall be able to extricate ourselves;<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"13149382141953598649\"><a href=\"#13149382141953598649\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans36\"><span id=\"documenttrans36\">36</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">In the months before JS wrote this letter, church leaders took on a large amount of debt in order to purchase land and acquire a new printing press and type to replace what had been damaged during the violence in <a href=\"/place/jackson-county-missouri\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Jackson County</a> on 20 July 1833. (See <a href=\"/paper-summary/minutes-23-march-1833-a\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/minutes-23-march-1833-a\" poptitle=\"Minutes, 23 March 1833–A\">Minutes, 23 Mar. 1833–A</a>; <a href=\"/paper-summary/minutes-11-september-1833\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/minutes-11-september-1833\" poptitle=\"Minutes, 11 September 1833\">Minutes, 11 Sept. 1833</a>; F. G. Williams and Company, Account Book, 1; Frederick G. Williams, Kirtland, OH, to “Dear Brethren,” 10 Oct. 1833, in <a href=\"/paper-summary/letterbook-1/68\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/letterbook-1\" poptitle=\"Letter, Frederick G. Williams to “Dear Brethren” • 10 October 1833\">JS Letterbook 1, pp. 56–60</a>; and Oliver Cowdery, Kirtland Mills, OH, to Ambrose Palmer, New Portage, OH, 30 Oct. 1833, in Cowdery, Letterbook, 8–9.)</p><hr><div class=\"works-cited-links\"><a href=\"/works-cited\" class=\"pdf\" id=\"comp-works\">Comprehensive Works Cited</a></div><div class=\"source-note\"><p id=\"source-note1\">F. G. Williams &amp; Co. Account Book, 1833–1835. CHL. In Patience Cowdery, Diary, 1849–1851. CHL. <a href=\"https://catalog.churchofjesuschrist.org/assets?id=e2604a66-1e72-42c1-8398-2f98dfc8a861&amp;crate=0&amp;index=0\" class=\"externalLink\">MS 3493</a>.</p><p id=\"source-note2\">Cowdery, Oliver. Letterbook, 1833–1838. Huntington Library, San Marino, CA.</p></div></div></div></aside> The inhabitants of this<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"8801853835679577351\"><a href=\"#8801853835679577351\" class=\"reference staticPopup\" ro=\"plc62\" place=\"plc62\" title=\"Geauga County, Ohio\"> county</a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data\" isnote=\"Y\" isnonsearchable=\"Y\"><input type=\"hidden\" value=\"Geauga County, Ohio\"><p>Located in northeastern Ohio, south of Lake Erie. Rivers in area include Grand, Chagrin, and Cuyahoga. Settled mostly by New Englanders, beginning 1798. Formed from Trumbull Co., 1 Mar. 1806. Chardon established as county seat, 1808. Population in 1830 about...</p><a class=\"more\" href=\"/place/geauga-county-ohio\">More Info</a></div></div></aside> [p. 68]</div></body></html>", "expandedText": "<html><head></head><body><div class=\"indent continuation wasptag\"> without,<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"6141584219657623072\"><a href=\"#6141584219657623072\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans26\"><span id=\"documenttrans26\">26</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">JS first made this point in a <a href=\"/paper-summary/letter-to-church-leaders-in-jackson-county-missouri-18-august-1833\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/letter-to-church-leaders-in-jackson-county-missouri-18-august-1833\" poptitle=\"Letter to Church Leaders in Jackson County,\nMissouri, 18 August 1833\">letter</a> dated 18 August 1833: “It is the will of the Lord that . . . not one foot of land perchased should be given to the enimies of God or sold to them.” JS dictated a <a href=\"/paper-summary/revelation-16-17-december-1833-dc-101\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/revelation-16-17-december-1833-dc-101\" poptitle=\"Revelation, 16–17 December 1833 [D&amp;C\n101]\">revelation</a> eleven days after this 5 December letter was written that gave the same directive: “It is my will that my people should claim and hold claim upon that which I have appointed unto them though they should not be permited to dwell thereon.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/letter-to-church-leaders-in-jackson-county-missouri-18-august-1833\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/letter-to-church-leaders-in-jackson-county-missouri-18-august-1833\" poptitle=\"Letter to Church Leaders in Jackson County,\nMissouri, 18 August 1833\">Letter to Church Leaders in Jackson Co., MO, 18 Aug. 1833</a>; <a href=\"/paper-summary/revelation-16-17-december-1833-dc-101\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/revelation-16-17-december-1833-dc-101\" poptitle=\"Revelation, 16–17 December 1833 [D&amp;C\n101]\">Revelation, 16–17 Dec. 1833</a> [D&amp;C 101:99].)</p><hr></div></div></aside> evry exertion should be to maintain <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>the cause you have espoused and to contribute to <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>the necessities of one another as much as possable <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>in this your great calamity and remember <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>not to murmur at the dealings of God with <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>his creature you are not as yet brought <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>into as trying circumstances as were the <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>ancient Prophets &amp; apostles Call to mind a <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>Daniel<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"16053426199031430599\"><a href=\"#16053426199031430599\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans27\"><span id=\"documenttrans27\">27</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Daniel 6:4–23.</p><hr></div></div></aside> the three Hebrew Children,<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"1831442725717443626\"><a href=\"#1831442725717443626\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans28\"><span id=\"documenttrans28\">28</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Daniel chap. 3.</p><hr></div></div></aside> Jeremiah<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"12594901367349407087\"><a href=\"#12594901367349407087\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans29\"><span id=\"documenttrans29\">29</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Lamentations 3:1–21.</p><hr></div></div></aside> <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>Paul<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"11269397666886267056\"><a href=\"#11269397666886267056\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans30\"><span id=\"documenttrans30\">30</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See 2 Corinthians 11:23–27.</p><hr></div></div></aside> Stephen<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"16436786247813344284\"><a href=\"#16436786247813344284\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans31\"><span id=\"documenttrans31\">31</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Acts 7:54–60.</p><hr></div></div></aside> and many more too numerous <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>to mention who were stoned sawn asunder <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>tempted slain with the sword and wandered <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>about in sheep skins &amp; goat skins being <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>destitute afflicted tormented of whom <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>the world was not worthy. they wandered <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>in deserts and in mountains and in dens <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>and caves of the earth yet they all obtained <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>a good report through faith<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"15066919719732989156\"><a href=\"#15066919719732989156\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans32\"><span id=\"documenttrans32\">32</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Hebrews 11:37–39.</p><hr></div></div></aside> and amidst all <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>their afflictions they rejoiced that they were <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>&lt;​counted​&gt; worthy to receive persecution for Christs sake<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"4361180339869302400\"><a href=\"#4361180339869302400\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans33\"><span id=\"documenttrans33\">33</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Acts 5:41.</p><hr></div></div></aside> <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>we know not what we shall be called to pass <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>through before <aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"1152057741016840366\"><a href=\"#1152057741016840366\" class=\"reference staticPopup\" ro=\"x4116\" topic=\"x4116\" title=\"Zion\">Zion</a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data\" isnote=\"Y\" isnonsearchable=\"Y\"><input type=\"hidden\" value=\"Zion\"><p>A specific location in Missouri; also a literal or figurative gathering of believers in Jesus Christ, characterized by adherence to ideals of harmony, equality, and purity. In JS’s earliest revelations “the cause of Zion” was used to broadly describe the ...</p><a class=\"more\" href=\"/topic/zion\">View Glossary</a></div></div></aside> is delivered and established <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>therefore we have great need to live near <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>to God and always be in strict obedience <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>to all his <aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"16533546041959748573\"><a href=\"#16533546041959748573\" class=\"reference staticPopup\" ro=\"x6221\" topic=\"x6221\" title=\"Commandment\">commandments</a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data\" isnote=\"Y\" isnonsearchable=\"Y\"><input type=\"hidden\" value=\"Commandment\"><p>Generally, a divine mandate that church members were expected to obey; more specifically, a text dictated by JS in the first-person voice of Deity that served to communicate knowledge and instruction to JS and his followers. Occasionally, other inspired texts...</p><a class=\"more\" href=\"/topic/commandment\">View Glossary</a></div></div></aside> that we may <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>have a concience void of offense towards <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>God and man,<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"1424040937669892326\"><a href=\"#1424040937669892326\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans34\"><span id=\"documenttrans34\">34</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Acts 24:16.</p><hr></div></div></aside> It is your privelege to <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>use every lawful means in your power <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>to seek redress for your grievances of your <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>enemies and prosecute them to the extent <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>of the Law<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"3884886235495565068\"><a href=\"#3884886235495565068\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans35\"><span id=\"documenttrans35\">35</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">See <a href=\"/paper-summary/letter-to-church-leaders-in-jackson-county-missouri-18-august-1833\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/letter-to-church-leaders-in-jackson-county-missouri-18-august-1833\" poptitle=\"Letter to Church Leaders in Jackson County,\nMissouri, 18 August 1833\">Letter to Church Leaders in Jackson Co., MO, 18 Aug. 1833</a>; and <a href=\"/paper-summary/revelation-16-17-december-1833-dc-101\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/revelation-16-17-december-1833-dc-101\" poptitle=\"Revelation, 16–17 December 1833 [D&amp;C\n101]\">Revelation, 16–17 Dec. 1833</a> [D&amp;C 101:81–89]; see also Historical Introduction to <a href=\"/paper-summary/letter-30-october-1833\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/letter-30-october-1833\" poptitle=\"Letter, 30 October 1833\">Letter, 30 Oct. 1833</a>.</p><hr></div></div></aside> but it will be impossable for us <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>to render you any assistance in a temporal <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>point of view as our means are already <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>exhausted and are deeply in debt and know <span class=\"line-break\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>no means whereby we shall be able to extri<span class=\"line-break hyphenate\" xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1999/xhtml\"></span>cate ourselves;<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"13503652339209131720\"><a href=\"#13503652339209131720\" class=\"editorial-note-static staticPopup\" title=\"Note\" name=\"trans36\"><span id=\"documenttrans36\">36</span></a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data editorial-footnote\" isnote=\"Y\"><p class=\"bottom_0\">In the months before JS wrote this letter, church leaders took on a large amount of debt in order to purchase land and acquire a new printing press and type to replace what had been damaged during the violence in <a href=\"/place/jackson-county-missouri\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Jackson County</a> on 20 July 1833. (See <a href=\"/paper-summary/minutes-23-march-1833-a\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/minutes-23-march-1833-a\" poptitle=\"Minutes, 23 March 1833–A\">Minutes, 23 Mar. 1833–A</a>; <a href=\"/paper-summary/minutes-11-september-1833\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/minutes-11-september-1833\" poptitle=\"Minutes, 11 September 1833\">Minutes, 11 Sept. 1833</a>; F. G. Williams and Company, Account Book, 1; Frederick G. Williams, Kirtland, OH, to “Dear Brethren,” 10 Oct. 1833, in <a href=\"/paper-summary/letterbook-1/68\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/letterbook-1\" poptitle=\"Letter, Frederick G. Williams to “Dear Brethren” • 10 October 1833\">JS Letterbook 1, pp. 56–60</a>; and Oliver Cowdery, Kirtland Mills, OH, to Ambrose Palmer, New Portage, OH, 30 Oct. 1833, in Cowdery, Letterbook, 8–9.)</p><hr><div class=\"works-cited-links\"><a href=\"/works-cited\" class=\"pdf\" id=\"comp-works\">Comprehensive Works Cited</a></div><div class=\"source-note\"><p id=\"source-note1\">F. G. Williams &amp; Co. Account Book, 1833–1835. CHL. In Patience Cowdery, Diary, 1849–1851. CHL. <a href=\"https://catalog.churchofjesuschrist.org/assets?id=e2604a66-1e72-42c1-8398-2f98dfc8a861&amp;crate=0&amp;index=0\" class=\"externalLink\">MS 3493</a>.</p><p id=\"source-note2\">Cowdery, Oliver. Letterbook, 1833–1838. Huntington Library, San Marino, CA.</p></div></div></div></aside> The inhabitants of this<aside class=\"popup-wrapper\" id=\"1292563583701068097\"><a href=\"#1292563583701068097\" class=\"reference staticPopup\" ro=\"plc62\" place=\"plc62\" title=\"Geauga County, Ohio\"> county</a><div class=\"popup-content\"><div class=\"note-data\" isnote=\"Y\" isnonsearchable=\"Y\"><input type=\"hidden\" value=\"Geauga County, Ohio\"><p>Located in northeastern Ohio, south of Lake Erie. Rivers in area include Grand, Chagrin, and Cuyahoga. Settled mostly by New Englanders, beginning 1798. Formed from Trumbull Co., 1 Mar. 1806. Chardon established as county seat, 1808. Population in 1830 about...</p><a class=\"more\" href=\"/place/geauga-county-ohio\">More Info</a></div></div></aside> [p. 68]</div></body></html>", "calendarsOfDocuments": [], "versions": [{"versionTitle": "Letter to Edward Partridge, 5 December 1833", "versionLink": "/paper-summary/letter-to-edward-partridge-5-december-1833/1", "versionFeatured": "true"}, {"versionTitle": "Letterbook 1", "versionLink": "/paper-summary/letterbook-1/77", "versionFeatured": ""}, {"versionTitle": "History, 1838–1856, volume A-1 [23 December 1805–30 August 1834]", "versionLink": "/paper-summary/history-1838-1856-volume-a-1-23-december-1805-30-august-1834/391", "versionFeatured": ""}, {"versionTitle": "“History of Joseph Smith”", "versionLink": "/paper-summary/history-of-joseph-smith/209", "versionFeatured": ""}], "images": {"smallImageUrl": "/7633/7633_ms155_2_01_lb1_068_thumb.jpg", "mediumImageUrl": "/7633/7633_ms155_2_01_lb1_068_med.jpg", "largeImageUrl": "/7633/7633_ms155_2_01_lb1_068.jpg", "downloadImageUrl": "/7633/7633_ms155_2_01_lb1_068_download.jpg"}, "imageCredit": "", "facsimile": "", "dzUrlXml": "/7633/7633_ms155_2_01_lb1_068.xml", "classification": "Documents", "publishedStatus": "FinalContent", "accessionNumber": "191", "requestedPageNumber": "4", "numberOfPages": "6", "editorialTitle": "Letter to Edward Partridge, 5 December 1833", "cleanEditorialTitle": "Letter to Edward Partridge, 5 December 1833", "date": "December 5, 1833", "handwriting": "Handwriting of Frederick G. Williams", "footnotes": [{"id": "6141584219657623072", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">JS first made this point in a <a href=\"/paper-summary/letter-to-church-leaders-in-jackson-county-missouri-18-august-1833\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/letter-to-church-leaders-in-jackson-county-missouri-18-august-1833\" poptitle=\"Letter to Church Leaders in Jackson County,\nMissouri, 18 August 1833\">letter</a> dated 18 August 1833: “It is the will of the Lord that . . . not one foot of land perchased should be given to the enimies of God or sold to them.” JS dictated a <a href=\"/paper-summary/revelation-16-17-december-1833-dc-101\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/revelation-16-17-december-1833-dc-101\" poptitle=\"Revelation, 16–17 December 1833 [D&amp;C\n101]\">revelation</a> eleven days after this 5 December letter was written that gave the same directive: “It is my will that my people should claim and hold claim upon that which I have appointed unto them though they should not be permited to dwell thereon.” (<a href=\"/paper-summary/letter-to-church-leaders-in-jackson-county-missouri-18-august-1833\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/letter-to-church-leaders-in-jackson-county-missouri-18-august-1833\" poptitle=\"Letter to Church Leaders in Jackson County,\nMissouri, 18 August 1833\">Letter to Church Leaders in Jackson Co., MO, 18 Aug. 1833</a>; <a href=\"/paper-summary/revelation-16-17-december-1833-dc-101\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/revelation-16-17-december-1833-dc-101\" poptitle=\"Revelation, 16–17 December 1833 [D&amp;C\n101]\">Revelation, 16–17 Dec. 1833</a> [D&amp;C 101:99].)</p></body></html>", "number": 26, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "16053426199031430599", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Daniel 6:4–23.</p></body></html>", "number": 27, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "1831442725717443626", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Daniel chap. 3.</p></body></html>", "number": 28, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "12594901367349407087", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Lamentations 3:1–21.</p></body></html>", "number": 29, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "11269397666886267056", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">See 2 Corinthians 11:23–27.</p></body></html>", "number": 30, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "16436786247813344284", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Acts 7:54–60.</p></body></html>", "number": 31, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "15066919719732989156", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Hebrews 11:37–39.</p></body></html>", "number": 32, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "4361180339869302400", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Acts 5:41.</p></body></html>", "number": 33, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "1424040937669892326", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">See Acts 24:16.</p></body></html>", "number": 34, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "3884886235495565068", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">See <a href=\"/paper-summary/letter-to-church-leaders-in-jackson-county-missouri-18-august-1833\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/letter-to-church-leaders-in-jackson-county-missouri-18-august-1833\" poptitle=\"Letter to Church Leaders in Jackson County,\nMissouri, 18 August 1833\">Letter to Church Leaders in Jackson Co., MO, 18 Aug. 1833</a>; and <a href=\"/paper-summary/revelation-16-17-december-1833-dc-101\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/revelation-16-17-december-1833-dc-101\" poptitle=\"Revelation, 16–17 December 1833 [D&amp;C\n101]\">Revelation, 16–17 Dec. 1833</a> [D&amp;C 101:81–89]; see also Historical Introduction to <a href=\"/paper-summary/letter-30-october-1833\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/letter-30-october-1833\" poptitle=\"Letter, 30 October 1833\">Letter, 30 Oct. 1833</a>.</p></body></html>", "number": 35, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": null}, {"id": "13503652339209131720", "text": "<html><head></head><body><p class=\"bottom_0\">In the months before JS wrote this letter, church leaders took on a large amount of debt in order to purchase land and acquire a new printing press and type to replace what had been damaged during the violence in <a href=\"/place/jackson-county-missouri\" class=\"referenceInPop\">Jackson County</a> on 20 July 1833. (See <a href=\"/paper-summary/minutes-23-march-1833-a\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/minutes-23-march-1833-a\" poptitle=\"Minutes, 23 March 1833–A\">Minutes, 23 Mar. 1833–A</a>; <a href=\"/paper-summary/minutes-11-september-1833\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers/tooltip/minutes-11-september-1833\" poptitle=\"Minutes, 11 September 1833\">Minutes, 11 Sept. 1833</a>; F. G. Williams and Company, Account Book, 1; Frederick G. Williams, Kirtland, OH, to “Dear Brethren,” 10 Oct. 1833, in <a href=\"/paper-summary/letterbook-1/68\" class=\"referenceInPop\" pop=\"/ajax/papers-text/tooltip/letterbook-1\" poptitle=\"Letter, Frederick G. Williams to “Dear Brethren” • 10 October 1833\">JS Letterbook 1, pp. 56–60</a>; and Oliver Cowdery, Kirtland Mills, OH, to Ambrose Palmer, New Portage, OH, 30 Oct. 1833, in Cowdery, Letterbook, 8–9.)</p></body></html>", "number": 36, "type": "number", "toenote": "", "longcitation": "<p id=\"source-note1\">F. G. Williams &amp; Co. Account Book, 1833–1835. CHL. In Patience Cowdery, Diary, 1849–1851. CHL. <a href=\"https://catalog.churchofjesuschrist.org/assets?id=e2604a66-1e72-42c1-8398-2f98dfc8a861&amp;crate=0&amp;index=0\" class=\"externalLink\">MS 3493</a>.</p><p id=\"source-note2\">Cowdery, Oliver. Letterbook, 1833–1838. Huntington Library, San Marino, CA.</p>"}], "meta": {"/paper-summary-PageTitle": "Letter to Edward Partridge, 5 December 1833, Page 68", "/paper-summary-MetaTitle": "Letter to Edward Partridge, 5 December 1833, Page 68", "/paper-summary-MetaDescription": "without,26JS first made this point in a letter dated 18 August 1833: “It is the will of the Lord tha...", "/paper-summary-MetaUrl": "/paper-summary/letter-to-edward-partridge-5-december-1833/4", "/paper-summary-MetaImage": "/bc-jsp/content/jsp/images/7633/7633_ms155_2_01_lb1_068_thumb.jpg"}, "scribes": [{"id": "per63", "name": "Frederick G. Williams"}], "printVolumeLocation": "<a href=\"http://www.josephsmithpapers.org/articles/documents-volume-3-february-1833-march-1834\" class=\"externalLink\" xmlns:srchdoc=\"https://ldsc.xmlref.com/local/ldschurch/searchDocuments\" xmlns:lds=\"https://ldsc.xmlref.com/local/ldschurch\"><span class=\